INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S COTTAGE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A storm rattles the shutters. A coal stove glows. On the table, a half-built model ship, a jar of glue, and a pot of cocoa gone cold.

PENNY ALDERWICK, 9, in her father's too-big wool sweater, kneels on a chair and fits a tiny mast into the deck. Her tongue sticks out in concentration.

A RADIO crackles on the shelf. A hiss of static, then a man's warm voice.

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Lighthouse base, this is the supply boat Gannet. Penny, you there?

She lunges for the radio. Presses the button.

                    PENNY
I'm here, Papa! Over!

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
How's the lamp?

                    PENNY
Turning. I counted. Forty-one seconds each time, just like you said.

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
That's my girl. Seas are rough. I'll be an hour late, maybe two. Keep the stove fed and stay put.

                    PENNY
Can I stay up?

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Until I'm home.

She grins. Sets the radio down gently, as if it might break.

A SHUTTER BANGS somewhere upstairs. Penny freezes. Looks at the ceiling.

                    PENNY
(whispering)
It's just wind.

She climbs down and lifts the oil lantern. Creeps to the stairs. The light throws her shadow huge on the wall.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE COTTAGE - STAIRS - CONTINUOUS

Each step creaks. At the top, a window hangs open. Rain whips in. The curtain flaps like a ghost.

She runs to it. Strains to pull the sash down. It sticks.

A SHADOW falls across the glass from outside.

Penny gasps. Backs away.

A face presses to the window, pale and wet. A knuckle raps the pane.

EZRA TULLY, 70s, drenched yellow oilskins, white beard dripping, gestures frantically at the latch. His boat lantern swings from one hand.

                    EZRA
(muffled)
Let me in, child! I'm half drowned!

Penny stares. Her lantern shakes.

                    PENNY
Papa says not to open the door to strangers.

                    EZRA
(muffled)
I'm no stranger! I'm the lamp-man from Skerry Point! Your father's friend!

                    PENNY
What's his name, then?

                    EZRA
(muffled)
Harold! He drinks his tea with a pinch of salt!

Penny's eyes widen. That's right. She hesitates, then yanks the latch.

The window swings wide. Ezra tumbles over the sill in a heap of dripping canvas, laughing and wheezing.

                    EZRA (CONT'D)
Bless you. My engine died a mile out. I rowed the whole way.

                    PENNY
You'd better come down. There's cocoa.

                    EZRA
Cold?

                    PENNY
Yes.

                    EZRA
Perfect. I'm too wet to be fussy.

The RADIO hisses downstairs. Faint, urgent.

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio, distant)
Penny? Penny, answer me!

She sprints down the stairs. Ezra follows, dripping, as fast as old knees allow.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE COTTAGE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

Penny grabs the radio and presses the button.

                    PENNY
Papa! I'm here! Somebody came! Mister Tully rowed over and he's all wet!

Static. Then the father's voice, tight.

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Who?

                    PENNY
Mister Tully. From Skerry Point. He knew about the salt.

A long pause. The storm howls.

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Penny. Ezra Tully died in March.

Penny turns, slowly. Ezra stands in the doorway, smiling gently. The water dripping from his coat makes no sound on the floor.

The lantern in her hand goes out.

                    PENNY
(barely a whisper)
Papa?

                    FATHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Stay where you are. Keep the lamp turning. I'm coming.

Above them, the great lamp swings its beam across the window. One flash. Ezra is gone. Only the wet footprints remain.

Penny holds the radio to her chest. Starts counting.

                    PENNY
One. Two. Three...
